[Approval Remarks] CS-013880|2026-11-03 16:40|Handled by: Community Operations Specialist (substitute)
Summary from the complainant: His account was restricted for "repeatedly posting despite serious misunderstanding of platform rules." During the appeal, he stated, "I thought only direct insults counted as violations, and saying the product was a scam should be fine." He did not submit any screenshots, screen recordings, or third-party materials throughout, nor did he specify which parts were missed in the original judgment.
Currently, the review criteria are used (three levels are combined for judgment, with item by item):
(1) If the appellant explicitly cites the original text of a rule and claims that the original judgment's interpretation was incorrect, and upon investigation, the clause cited in the original judgment has already taken effect on the date of publication and has been publicly posted on the site for at least 72 hours, this constitutes a misunderstanding of the rule and the original judgment is upheld;
(2) If the original judgment lacks context, it should be handled by whether the specific missing interval is specified (not specified in this case, so not applicable);
(3) If irony is claimed, the original sentence must contain an ironic mark (not present in this case and not applicable);
(4) If submitting counter-evidence, the materials must be verifiable and able to directly overturn the facts of the original judgment (if there is no material in this case, it is not applicable);
(5) If none of the above four categories are established, it is considered as the absence of substantive reasons and the original judgment is upheld.
Comparison: The clause cited in the original judgment came into effect and was publicly announced for at least 72 hours before 2026-11-25. The appellant's claim of "thinking" is a self-infusive assumption of the clause's scope, not a problem of the clause itself, falling under the elements of (1). However, the appeal text does not cite the original clause, so it fits better with (5).
Conclusion: Upholding the original verdict; The grasping is set as "high," citing incomplete requirements for the claim and still leaving one window for supplementary materials.
